      "title": "AI-related hiring shifts boost data-center trades but squeeze entry-level jobs",
      "content_html": "Data-center construction is increasing demand for skilled trades, while employers in some AI-exposed fields are reducing or delaying entry-level hiring rather than making widespread cuts to existing staff. Builders in western Pennsylvania say data-center and natural-gas projects need electricians, plumbers, carpenters and other tradespeople. Research cited by Index.hr found weaker hiring among young workers in exposed occupations, with U.S. employment among 22- to 25-year-olds 19% below the comparison trend.\n\nGartner forecasts that AI could eliminate 8.9 million jobs and create 10.6 million worldwide by 2030, a net increase of 1.7 million; it also estimates 435 million workers could see major changes to their jobs. OECD and Eurofound analyses cited by Index.hr caution that youth employment had weakened before AI's widespread adoption and found no distinct AI-related decline among young workers across the EU.\n\nA 2026 study found junior software vacancies fell almost 15% relative to senior vacancies after generative AI became publicly available, but did not establish that AI caused the change. Data-center projects are also generating construction work in Nevada, where union members are divided over the trade-off between construction jobs and potential service-sector job losses.",
